feat: a project's source comes with the document, read lazily

Testing through the live host showed the gap: `open('ags-kdmapper-1909')` returned
an 8-line description, while the 492 KB source dump beside it — the thing actually
worth working on — needed a separate paginated `read_source` call.

A document now carries its source:

    const d = await open('ags-kdmapper-1909')
    d.hasSource                        true
    await d.sourceSize()               { bytes: 492572, lines: 12466 }
    await d.sourceLines(0, 200)        a range
    await d.grepSource(/iqvw64e/i)     line-numbered matches

STREAMED, NOT LOADED. The archive's largest dump is 100 MB (315,920 lines), so
every access streams line by line. Measured: a ranged read takes 288ms and a
search 307ms, with the heap at 21 MB — a `readFileSync` would have put 206 MB in
the process to answer the same question.

A capped search says how many matched: `... 304888 more of 304891 matches`. A
skill with no dump answers `hasSource: false`, and asking anyway refuses with the
reason rather than returning nothing.

THE GATE CAUGHT ITS OWN HARNESS BUG, which is the part worth recording. Its first
version measured the checker's memory, not the server's — the server is a
separate process, so it could never see the growth. It now reads a `stats()`
helper the harness exposes (heap, peak, kept, sessions), and `peakMB` is sampled
at the end of each call, before the collector can reclaim a large temporary.

AND A WORSE TRAP, now closed: two gate runs reported `gate-passed` for code that
was not there. A broken injection made the build fail, the old `dist` stayed in
place, and the gate tested the previous artifact. A gate against a stale build is
not a gate, so the battery now refuses to run when any source is newer than
`dist/index.js`, with a message naming the files and the fix.

Found by driving the host, not by the suite: 15 gates and 49 tests were green
while `open()` gave a model a stub and hid the source.
